            "A shared institution may constrain unilateral action, yet it can create a new concentration of power in the authority that operates it. If one party controls the secretariat, data platform, funding stream, appointment process, or emergency switch, the arrangement may preserve the appearance of joint ownership while centralizing effective control. The audit should map actual decision rights rather than rely on labels such as joint, mutual, or neutral.",
            "An institution's mandate must be bounded. It should be clear which matters the body may decide, which it may only advise on, and which remain with the states or affected communities. The body should not gain authority through technical practice that was never granted by the treaty. If its role expands, the parties should use the agreed amendment or delegation procedure and document the new basis.",
            "Representation is one safeguard but not the only one. A joint board may have equal seats while one side supplies the technical staff and controls the budget. A rotating chair may appear balanced while agenda-setting remains fixed. Meaningful participation requires timely access to records, opportunities to propose issues, independent advice, and a process for dissent to be recorded. The parties should examine how these rights work in ordinary meetings and emergencies.",
            "The institution should also have an accountability route external to itself. Depending on the arrangement, this could include reports to the parties, independent financial audit, judicial review, legislative scrutiny, or a public complaints procedure. The choice should fit the institution's legal status and confidentiality requirements. An authority that is accountable only to its own staff cannot credibly claim to serve the parties as a whole.",
            "A continuity plan should address staff turnover, cyber or physical disruption, budget interruption, and contested appointments. Records should remain available to both parties, and no single administrator should be able to erase the history of decisions. Replacement procedures should prevent a vacancy from paralyzing an essential function while protecting the integrity of the selection process.",
            "Finally, shared infrastructure should be assessed for unequal consequences if cooperation stops. If one party can continue operating while the other loses essential access, a nominally joint asset may create a dependency that can be used as leverage. Parties may need alternate access, data portability, emergency service guarantees, or a neutral transition procedure. Such protections do not eliminate interdependence; they keep it from becoming an unreviewable instrument of domination.",
            "The Dominium lens is therefore strongest when it asks who holds the keys, who can inspect the ledger, who can pause an operation, who can appeal, and who bears the consequence of failure. These are concrete questions that can be answered from the instrument and its practice. They are more informative than a general assurance that custody is mutual.",
