            "A compact review of a proposed custodial arrangement can ask five questions. What specific right, asset, or process is being administered, and who holds the underlying legal authority? Which institution exercises day-to-day control, and how is that mandate limited? What evidence allows each party to assess performance? Which forum can review a disputed decision, and what interim safeguard applies? Finally, what lawful process governs amendment, suspension, or exit?",
            "The answers should be stated separately for each party. If one side supplies the asset and the other controls access, their risks differ. If one party is responsible for a duty but has no access to the data needed to perform it, the arrangement is operationally unbalanced. If a body can impose a consequence without giving reasons or permitting review, the body itself may become a source of coercion.",
