            "The ECSC should not be described as a system of equal state voting or as an institution that prevented large states from exercising influence. Its treaty created distinct institutions with different membership and functions: the High Authority acted within powers conferred by the treaty, the Council represented member governments, the Common Assembly brought parliamentary representatives together, and the Court interpreted the treaty within its jurisdiction. Institutional balance arose from this combination and from legally specified competences, not from a single rule of equal votes in every decision.",
