            "The historical cases sharpen rather than settle that inquiry. Versailles shows why text, later implementation, and reception must be separated; the ECSC shows how shared institutions can influence incentives without eliminating power or replacing other security arrangements; and the Gothic crossing shows how source criticism changes the strength of a historical analogy. The operator's task is to use these cases to generate questions, verify claims against primary records, and recommend safeguards that fit the actual agreement. A measured conclusion is not a weaker conclusion: it is one that can be tested, challenged, and revised in light of evidence.",
